From 68af1a5e9b5b82a12de01d3b8941cc57ba8a4367 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeffrey Frederick <5095066+jeffreyfrederick@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 20:55:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Cache shadow geometry and draw view paths in local space

`draw_shadows` rebuilt every shadow path each frame and, for inset
shadows, ran an `SkPathOps` difference per shadow per frame. Paths were
also positioned with `make_offset`, which creates a new `SkPath` with a
new generation ID every frame, so Skia's GPU caches (triangulated
vertex buffers, blur masks), which are keyed by path identity, never
hit: every frame re-triangulated each shadow, allocated new GPU vertex
buffers and kept the GL driver busy.

- Cache each view's shadow layers (path + paint) in view-local space in
  `CachedData::shadows`, keyed by the resolved shadow list and scale
  factor. Invalidate together with the cached outline path through a
  new `CachedData::invalidate_path` (all former `cache.path.remove`
  sites).
- Draw shadows and the background colour from the cached local outline
  under a canvas translation instead of offsetting paths.
- `DrawContext::path()` keeps its behaviour (absolute coordinates).

Rendering: a pixel diff of a 1440x900 frame before/after differs in
2.6k pixels, all on anti-aliased edges of small rounded shadow rings
(max 16/255 per channel; corners are now slightly smoother because Skia
can use its cached path renderers). Shadow transitions still work: the
cache simply misses while a transition is running.

Tested on the ChipDAW GUI prototype (about 30 inset + 30 outer shadows
redrawn per frame during playback), Windows/NVIDIA, 60 Hz:
process CPU 59.5% -> 24.3% (43-clip demo playing), 96.8% -> 50.5%
(200 clips playing).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
